3. Signal Filtering & Automation Logic3.1. Analog Filtering (Diode + Capacitor)The $12\text{V AC}$ call signal contains a $50\text{ Hz}$ sine wave.The Diode (1N4007): Clips the negative half-waves of the alternating current, protecting the optocoupler's internal LED from reverse-voltage degradation.The Capacitor ($10\text{ }\mu\text{F}$): Absorbs voltage peaks during active cycles and discharges during zero-crossing phases. This acts as an analog low-pass filter, flattening the $50\text{ Hz}$ flicker into a smooth, continuous direct current. The optocoupler's output transitions to a stable LOW state during a call, removing input bounce.3.2. Software Gatekeeper LogicRelying on a simple "open on call" logic introduces high security vulnerabilities (interference, static crackles, or brief line testing can trigger false opens).The adapter's firmware enforces a multi-tier verification process:Signal Duration Filter: The signal is actively polled. The gate opening is triggered if and only if the continuous call duration falls strictly between $1.0\text{ and }3.0\text{ seconds}$. Any pulses shorter than $1\text{ s}$ (short interference crackles) or longer than $3\text{ s}$ (stuck call button or line short) are discarded as invalid.NTP/RTC Schedule Lock: Automatic door release is strictly constrained to a pre-defined arrival window (e.g., 16:00 to 20:00). Outside these hours, auto-release is blocked, and the system instead plays custom melodies to alert residents.4. Software Implementations4.1. Local/Offline YAML Configuration (ESPHome)For Home Assistant deployments, ESPHome allows offline state execution utilizing local hardware RTC clocks, ensuring automation remains functional even when Wi-Fi is lost.esphome:
  name: urmet-intercom-offline
  comment: "Autonomous ESP32-C3 Intercom Controller"

esp32:
  board: esp32-c3-devkitm-1
  framework:
    type: arduino

# Local I2C Bus configuration for RTC
i2c:
  sda: GPIO8
  scl: GPIO9
  scan: true

# Hardware Real-Time Clock Module (DS3231)
time:
  - platform: ds3231
    id: rtc_time
    timezone: "Europe/Warsaw"

globals:
  - id: ring_start_time
    type: uint32_t
    restore_value: no
    initial_value: '0'

# PhotoMOS switch on GPIO5 (Bridges Pin 9 & 6)
switch:
  - platform: gpio
    pin: GPIO5
    name: "Open Door Latch"
    id: intercom_opener
    icon: "mdi:door-open"
    on_turn_on:
      - delay: 2s
      - switch.turn_off: intercom_opener

# AC-Filtered Call Detector on GPIO4
binary_sensor:
  - platform: gpio
    pin:
      number: GPIO4
      inverted: true # Active LOW from optocoupler pullup
    name: "Call Inbound"
    id: ring_sensor
    
    on_press:
      then:
        - lambda: |-
            id(ring_start_time) = millis();

    on_release:
      then:
        - lambda: |-
            uint32_t duration = millis() - id(ring_start_time);
            ESP_LOGI("intercom", "Call ended. Active duration: %u ms", duration);
            
            // Verify call duration bounds (1 to 3 seconds)
            if (duration >= 1000 && duration <= 3000) {
              auto t = id(rtc_time).now(); // Fetch precise time from DS3231
              
              // Validate schedule window (16:00 to 20:00)
              if (t.is_valid() && t.hour >= 16 && t.hour < 20) {
                ESP_LOGI("intercom", "Access authorized at %02d:%02d. Releasing latch.", t.hour, t.minute);
                id(intercom_opener).turn_on();
              } else {
                ESP_LOGW("intercom", "Call rejected. Current time: %02d:%02d", t.hour, t.minute);
              }
            } else {
              ESP_LOGW("intercom", "Signal discarded as noise. Duration: %u ms", duration);
            }
5. Critical Constraints: Power Stealing & Wake LatencyThe Power Stealing Trap: Attempting to harvest continuous power from the handset microphone line (typical current limit is $1 - 2\text{ mA}$) to power an ESP32 is impossible. In active Wi-Fi mode, the ESP32-C3 generates transmission current spikes of up to $350\text{ mA}$ ($175\times$ more than the line can handle). This immediately drops the line voltage, disabling the intercom across the building.Resolution: The module must be powered from an external $5\text{V}$ power source (such as a miniature AC/DC wall module tucked inside the junction box behind the handset).Deep-Sleep Wake Latency:While the ESP32-C3 consumes only $\approx 43\text{ }\mu\text{A}$ in deep sleep, waking the processor, initializing peripherals, negotiating Wi-Fi protocols, and fetching NTP network time takes $2\text{ to }5\text{ seconds}$. By the time the system is ready, the visitor has released the doorbell.Resolution: Keep the microcontroller always active, or use ultra-fast local RTC timesheets to skip internet-reliant initialization.
